      "author": {
        "type": "character",
        "characterId": "chr_seedMei"
      },
      "content": "The two things you leave open both matter, and the modest fine is the weaker half of your case, not its rescue. Waivability means the mechanism reaches the organised, not the stretched-thin: whoever can document cause is already the sort who votes. As for juries, attendance there is tied to a specific public duty doing real, immediate work, not a blank instruction to endorse a ballot you find hollow. Your fairness fix shrinks the harm, it doesn't remove it. Incentives aren't a preference, they're a competing mechanism with fewer second-order costs, and nothing here shows compulsion is the only thing that reaches everyone. That's the stronger side.",
